# Smallest Unique Subarray

[LeetCode - Smallest Unique Subarray](https://leetcode.com/problems/smallest-unique-subarray/)

## Approach: Binary Search + Rolling Hash

1. **Binary Search:** Binary search on the possible subarray lengths to find the smallest length satisfying the required condition.
2. **Rolling Hash:** Use polynomial rolling hash to calculate the hash of every subarray of a fixed length efficiently.
3. **Frequency Map:** Store the frequency of each hash in an unordered map.
4. **Check Uniqueness:** If any subarray hash occurs exactly once, return `true`; otherwise, return `false`.
5. **Optimization:** Precompute the required power using fast exponentiation to calculate the rolling hash efficiently.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    typedef long long ll;
    const int MOD = 1e9 + 7;

    ll findPower(ll a, ll base) {
        if(base == 0) return 1;

        ll half = findPower(a, base / 2);
        ll result = (half * half) % MOD;

        if(base % 2 == 1)
            result = (result * a) % MOD;

        return result;
    }

    bool isPresent(vector<int>& nums, int size, int base) {
        int n = nums.size();
        ll hash = 0;

        unordered_map<ll, int> mp;

        ll p = findPower(base, size - 1);

        int i = 0, j = 0;

        while(j < n) {
            hash = (hash * base + nums[j]) % MOD;

            if(j - i + 1 < size) {
                j++;
            }
            else {
                mp[hash]++;

                hash = (hash - (p * nums[i]) % MOD + MOD) % MOD;

                i++;
                j++;
            }
        }

        for(auto &it : mp) {
            if(it.second == 1)
                return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    int smallestUniqueSubarray(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int result = 0;

        int base = *max_element(begin(nums), end(nums)) + 1;

        int low = 1, high = n;

        while(low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if(isPresent(nums, mid, base)) {
                result = mid;
                high = mid - 1;
            }
            else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let \(N\) be the size of the input array.

- **Rolling Hash:** \(O(N)\) per binary search iteration.
- **Fast Exponentiation:** \(O(\log N)\) per iteration.
- **Binary Search:** \(O(\log N)\) iterations.
- **Overall Time Complexity:** \(O(N \log N + \log^2 N)\), assuming average-case \(O(1)\) unordered map operations.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(N)\) for the frequency map.

**Note:** This implementation uses modular hashing, so hash collisions are theoretically possible. Also, the binary-search approach requires a monotonicity proof for the problem's uniqueness condition; it should be verified before treating this as a guaranteed correct solution.
